\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
\label{sec:future-work}

This work develops a three-dimensional quaternion MEKF in which a latent
Ornstein--Uhlenbeck acceleration prior drives velocity, oscillatory displacement,
and an integral-displacement state whose zero pseudo-measurement provides
low-frequency drift control.  The Live stability analysis follows the source
modes directly.  While accelerometer-bias learning is held, the active 18-state
subsystem is uniformly exponentially stable under the stated translational and
finite-window attitude/gyro-bias information conditions, with the frozen bias
entering as a bounded input.  After bias release, the active 21-state recursion
is UES under either augmented attitude/gyro/accelerometer-bias excitation or the
finite-correlation-time bias fallback.  The deployed positive OU process floor
and exact OU-driven integration chain provide the required active translational
process excitation analytically.  Without an absolute heading reference, the
same structure descends to the yaw quotient rather than assigning convergence
to absolute yaw.

The nonlinear Live result is expressed in the quantities that drive the MEKF
geometry: attitude error, mixed attitude--bias and attitude--latent-acceleration
products, and the actual gain-weighted attitude correction.  A time-varying
quadratic Lyapunov metric converts the active-mode UES result into a practical
ISS tube without requiring one common small Euclidean radius for
$S$, $a_w$, $b_a$, velocity, and position.

Startup is quantitative.  For the measurement-only Mahony gravity-direction
observer with integral gyro-bias feedback, Sec.~\ref{sec:semiglobal-proxy}
derives an explicit practical-ISS rate, input gain, tilt envelope, and
observable-bias envelope on a compact subset of the almost-global basin.  The
same estimate gives a calculable sufficient gravity-gate entry time and an
analytical timeout attitude envelope.  The accepted source handoff is propagated
as its actual compact set $\mathcal H$, not as a mandatory outer-radius box.
Verified exact-map reachability permits transient growth while all prefixes
remain in the safe domain and establishes finite entry into the robust inner
regional set.

A timeout with a heading gauge uses the same direct full-heading capture
construction.  An ungauged timeout instead receives a regional practical-ISS
result on the yaw quotient; a later magnetic acquisition selects a horizontal
representative and enters the full-heading chain only when its exact jump image
and recovery flow remain certified.  Magnetic gauge changes and Live tilt
re-locks are explicit hybrid jumps.  They may enlarge the error while remaining
inside the proof-safe domain.  Finite jump sequences are followed by finite
recovery, and recurrent tilt re-locks admit a practical exponential bound when
the source cooldown and jump/flow gains satisfy the dwell-time small-gain
condition of Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}.  Continuous hard-iron
correction writes no attitude or covariance and remains part of the bounded
normal-Live measurement schedule.

The adaptation layer separates sea time scale, OU-prior amplitude, and integral
regularization.  The measured sea period sets $\tau$, the physical band-limited
acceleration RMS is mapped to $\sigma_{aw}$, and the $S=0$ pseudo-measurement is
set independently by the reduced physical-MSE balance
\begin{equation}
r_S^\star\propto
q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{1/14}m_{-4}^{3/7}T_S^{-1/2}.
\end{equation}
For a fixed-shape sea with
$m_{-4}\propto\sigma_a^2\tau^8$, strong-observation
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ independent of sea amplitude to leading order, and
$T_S\propto\tau$, this becomes
\begin{equation}
\boxed{r_S^\star\propto\sigma_a^{6/7}\tau^{41/14}.}
\end{equation}
The exact spectral evaluation reproduces these exponents over the reference
spectra and gives the deployed coefficient $C_J\simeq0.0538$.

Under the paired ten-seed stationary JONSWAP protocol, normalized vertical RMS
displacement error is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$ for OU--III versus
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II, with a paired difference of
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage points and bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$.
PM--Stokes gives the same qualitative vertical result.  Mean 3-D displacement
RMS is also lower for the current isotropic OU--III configuration in each
stationary JONSWAP sea and in the controlled transition, with paired bootstrap
intervals excluding zero.  These results apply to the tested directional and
spectral families, not to absolute navigation.

The horizontal analysis explains why isotropic regularization can remain useful
despite much larger low-frequency horizontal acceleration error.  The scalar
spectral reduction identifies how posterior acceleration-error intensity,
pseudo-measurement covariance, and cadence jointly place the low-frequency
correction corner, but it does not determine universal world-axis gains in the
presence of attitude coupling and heading-dependent orbital motion.  Reducing
tilt error, improving accelerometer-bias observability, or adding horizontal
aiding remains a more direct route to reducing the horizontal disturbance.  The
observed $x/y$ split is heading dependent and does not support universal
world-axis tuning constants.

The remaining analytical gap is the complete 21-state physical error problem.
The reduced model does not retain attitude/gravity leakage, gyroscope and
accelerometer bias paths, OU-model mismatch, cross-covariance coupling, or the
full periodic update sequence.  Section~\ref{sec:adaptation-full-state-h2}
formulates that physical displacement objective and provides the natural next
step for assessing how those couplings modify the reduced SpectralMSE exponents
and coefficient.

The main limitations are the primarily simulation-based evidence, comparison
centered on OU--II, controlled rather than general changing-sea case,
fixed-temperature study, lack of physical off-CoG lever-arm validation, and
incomplete magnetic-distortion model.  The analytical startup and timeout bounds
remain conditional on supplied physical disturbance, seed, heading, and
non-attitude state bounds.  Motion-dependent attitude/bias excitation, exact
source-shaped regional reachability, stochastic drift, and hybrid jump/flow
gains remain certificate obligations until they are numerically instantiated
for a declared operating envelope.  The ESP32--S3 exercise establishes source
portability but not processor, memory, power, or deadline margins.  Reported
displacement remains bounded wave motion about a local reference; no global
navigation position is claimed without external aiding.

\subsection*{Future work}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.25em]
  \item \textbf{Full-state physical $H_2$ analysis.}  Solve the periodic
        21-state Riccati and physical-error recursions of
        Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-full-state-h2} at the calibrated sea states and
        compare their predicted minimizing amplitudes and local exponents with
        the deployed SpectralMSE $6/7,41/14$ law and complete-estimator behavior.
  \item \textbf{Regional certificate instantiation.}  Verify the
        motion-dependent PE margins, source-shaped handoff reachability,
        structured nonlinear tube, stochastic drift, quotient capture, and
        hybrid jump/flow inequalities for the deployed source family.
  \item \textbf{Transition-aware regularizer amplitude.}  If the full-state
        model predicts a stationary--transition conflict outside the currently
        tested transition, preserve the steady-state regularizer mapping and
        test asymmetric or rate-limited response during changing seas.
  \item \textbf{Reference-point and GNSS validation.}  Validate the lever-arm
        model at a surveyed nonzero offset and add GNSS-aided separation of slow
        absolute trajectory from bounded wave motion, including asynchronous
        timing, outages, and reacquisition.
  \item \textbf{Horizontal observability.}  Quantify how GNSS aiding, improved
        tilt information, and accelerometer-bias observability reduce the
        low-frequency horizontal acceleration-error density identified in
        Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits}.
  \item \textbf{Vessel-response realism.}  Apply hull response-amplitude
        operators to the directional sea and include off-CoG rotational
        acceleration so validation distinguishes ocean forcing from vessel
        response.
  \item \textbf{Multimodal and multidirectional seas.}  Test crossing seas,
        independent swell and wind-sea components, multiple peaks, heading
        changes, and time-varying directional spectra.
  \item \textbf{Wind-heel separation.}  Validate the companion slow-equilibrium
        heel model with apparent wind and vessel motion, including long swell,
        asymmetric loading, sensor lever-arm motion, and gust transients.
  \item \textbf{Temperature-dependent bias.}  Evaluate temperature variation,
        calibration error, thermal lag, hysteresis, and residual-bias
        correlation time against measured temperature histories.
  \item \textbf{Magnetic calibration under heading excitation.}  Use
        multi-heading data to identify soft-iron and sensor-misalignment terms
        beyond the present additive hard-iron model.
  \item \textbf{Independent physical validation and embedded margins.}  Repeat
        the paired protocol against an external displacement reference or
        commercial MRU and measure execution time, memory, power, and deadline
        margin across update rates and sensor configurations.
\end{itemize}